1.95}, "rating_margin": 4.3, "sigma_race": 5.8, "sigma_total": 6.46, "baseline_margin": 6.53, "analyst_adjustment": -0.03, "key_factors": ["Ager's fundraising dominance ($5.9M raised vs. $400K for Balkcom) and the Trump super PAC canceling $1M+ in ads backing Balkcom", "Open seat in an R+5 district during a strongly favorable Democratic environment", "Emerson's mid-October poll showed Ager +13 among likely voters; markets price Ager near 85%", "Late-October ratings shifted toward Lean D (flip), with two outlets at Likely D"], "rationale": "The open seat pairs a Republican-leaning district with a strongly Democratic environment, and Ager's roughly 15-to-1 fundraising edge plus the cancellation of Republican outside advertising have shifted momentum his way. A mid-October Emerson poll showed him up 13, prediction markets price him near 85 percent, and most raters now call the race Lean Democratic.", "watch": "A late resurgence of Republican outside spending, Libertarian vote drawing from Ager, or renewed traction for the attack-ad controversy could narrow the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Chuck Edwards (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "NC-11's open seat pits Democrat Jamie Ager against Republican Jennifer Balkcom after incumbent Chuck Edwards withdrew following renomination. The western North Carolina district leans Republican by about five points, but the national environment favors Democrats this year, and Ager has built a large financial advantage, raising nearly $5.9 million to Balkcom's roughly $400,000. A Trump-aligned super PAC canceled more than $1 million in planned ads supporting Balkcom in late October.\n\nAn Emerson College poll in mid-October put Ager ahead by 13 points among likely voters, though a September survey from a Democratic-leaning firm showed a much narrower race. Rating outlets have mostly moved the contest to Lean Democratic, with some calling it a tossup, and prediction markets price Ager's chances near 85 pe